**Merge sort vs quicksort.**
- **Merge sort:** divide in half, sort each, merge. O(n log n) in every case, **stable**, but needs O(n) extra space for arrays (good for linked lists and external sorting).
- **Quicksort:** pick a pivot, partition into smaller and larger, recurse. Average O(n log n), worst case O(n^2) (bad pivots), **in place** (O(log n) stack if you recurse into the smaller side first), not stable, and usually faster in practice due to cache behaviour. Mitigate bad pivots with a **random pivot** or median-of-three; handle many duplicates with **three-way partitioning**.

Quicksort is shorter to write on a whiteboard, which is why it is a sensible choice here.

**Implementation (C++, Lomuto partition, random pivot).**

```cpp
int partitionRange(vector<int>& a, int lo, int hi) {
    int p = lo + rand() % (hi - lo + 1);
    swap(a[p], a[hi]);              // move pivot to the end
    int pivot = a[hi], i = lo;      // i = next slot for an element < pivot
    for (int j = lo; j < hi; ++j)
        if (a[j] < pivot) swap(a[i++], a[j]);
    swap(a[i], a[hi]);              // pivot into its final place
    return i;
}

void quickSort(vector<int>& a, int lo, int hi) {
    while (lo < hi) {
        int p = partitionRange(a, lo, hi);
        if (p - lo < hi - p) { quickSort(a, lo, p - 1); lo = p + 1; }
        else                 { quickSort(a, p + 1, hi); hi = p - 1; }
    }
}
// call: quickSort(a, 0, (int)a.size() - 1);
```

**Explain as you write.** After partition, everything left of index `p` is `< pivot`, everything right is `>= pivot`, and `a[p]` is in its final position. Recursing on the smaller part and looping on the larger bounds stack depth to O(log n).

**Test aloud:** empty array, one element, all equal, already sorted, reverse sorted, negative numbers.

**Complexity:** time O(n log n) expected, O(n^2) worst; space O(log n).

**Mistakes to avoid:** off-by-one in the loop bounds, using the first element as pivot without comment on sorted input, and forgetting to move the pivot to its final position.
